From ac42c32b79ec542d0a0bf338126cbe33c0f737d0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 18:43:09 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] compression: an LZ4 image's blocks are its chunks, not
 its rows
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

spice-server compresses each QXLDataChunk of the source bitmap in one
LZ4 call, so the number of blocks is the number of chunks and each one
holds however many rows that chunk had. A bitmap small enough to be one
chunk — which a 1024x768 desktop is — arrives as a single block holding
all 768 rows.

Decoding a block per row therefore fails on the first one: lz4_flex is
given a row's worth of output space for a whole image's worth of data
and returns "provided output is too small". The decoder used to break
out of the loop there with the RGBA buffer still zeroed and return Some,
a black rectangle nothing above it could tell apart; now that it is all
or nothing it refuses the image instead, and every such image decodes
to None.

Decoding block by block until the bitmap is full covers both shapes,
since a chunk boundary is always a row boundary. The cap handed to
lz4_flex is what is left of the image rather than a whole one, so a
block that claims more than the bitmap holds is still refused. A
payload that runs out before the bitmap is full, a block past its end
or an LZ4 error fail the whole image, as upstream's decoder does: a
short image is refused rather than returned with its missing rows black.

Checked against qemu 10.1 with `-spice image-compression=lz4`: the
desktop is now identical, pixel for pixel, to the same desktop under
GLZ, where before it was blank.

